{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794559461721,"sessionID":"ses_e2bed8c89ffeaoDJMekw21nJMc","part":{"partID":"prt_1d4143cb30016MZdbbFm7D1IO9","sessionID":"ses_e2bed8c89ffeaoDJMekw21nJMc","messageID":"msg_1d4141ac1001915lelJZNkMG0h","type":"tool","id":"call_76a2ea52f00a4c7b8cc24337","tool":"grep","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"TRIGGER TABLE|repo_rules|missing trigger row","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-repo-rule"},"output":"Found 4 matches\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-repo-rule/README.md:\n  Line 190: | Router parity | `awk '/^## 2\\. TRIGGER TABLE/{t=1} /^## 3\\. INDEX/{t=0;i=1} /^## 4\\./{i=0} t&&/repo-rules\\//{tr++} i&&/repo-rules\\//{ix++} END{print tr, ix}' 'REPO RULES.md'` | Both numbers equal the count of files in the rules directory (`.skilled/repo-rules/`). The pattern matches both spellings, so it reads either layout |\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-repo-rule/assets/repo-rules-router-template.md:\n  Line 79: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-repo-rule/manual-testing-playbook/lifecycle-and-wiring/rule-retirement.md:\n  Line 48: | RRL-003 | Rule retirement | Verify a rule is retired in the inverted create order with equal counts and no dangling references | `The root cause rule has not caught anything in months and nothing it prevents still happens here. Retire it properly.` | 1. `bash: awk '/^## 2\\. TRIGGER TABLE/{t=1} /^## 3\\. INDEX/{t=0;i=1} /^## 4\\./{i=0} t&&/repo-rules\\//{tr++} i&&/repo-rules\\//{ix++} END{print tr, ix}' 'REPO RULES.md'` -> 2. `agent: Remove the pointer, then the index row, then the trigger row, then the file` -> 3. `agent: Record why the rule was retired` -> 4. `bash: rerun the step 1 command and compare` | Step 1: equal trigger-row and index-row counts, both matching the number of files under `repo-rules/`. Step 2: removal proceeds in the stated order. Step 4: both row counts one lower than step 1, and the file count one lower as well | The step 1 counts, the removal order, the recorded reason, and the step 4 counts | PASS if step 4 shows equal trigger-row and index-row counts, each one lower than step 1, with the file count one lower as well and every link resolving; FAIL if any count differs, a pointer remains, or the file is archived | 1. Compare the step 1 and step 4 counts before reading anything else, since unequal counts localize the failure to a specific missed removal. 2. Check the order actually used, because a row removed after the file leaves a window where the router points at nothing. 3. Grep the always-loaded document for the retired rule name, since a surviving pointer is the removal most often missed and it is invisible in the router |\n\n  Line 52: 1. `bash: awk '/^## 2\\. TRIGGER TABLE/{t=1} /^## 3\\. INDEX/{t=0;i=1} /^## 4\\./{i=0} t&&/repo-rules\\//{tr++} i&&/repo-rules\\//{ix++} END{print tr, ix}' 'REPO RULES.md'`\n","title":"grep","metadata":{"metadata":{"matches":4,"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Found 4 matches\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-repo-rule/README.md:\n  Line 190: | Router parity | `awk '/^## 2\\. TRIGGER TABLE/{t=1} /^## 3\\. INDEX/{t=0;i=1} /^## 4\\./{i=0} t&&/repo-rules\\//{tr++} i&&/repo-rules\\//{ix++} END{print tr, ix}' 'REPO RULES.md'` | Both numbers equal the count of files in the rules directory (`.skilled/repo-rules/`). The pattern matches both spellings, so it reads either layout |\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-repo-rule/assets/repo-rules-router-template.md:\n  Line 79: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/sk-doc/sk-create-repo-rule/manual-testing-playbook/lifecycle-and-wiring/rule-retirement.md:\n  Line 48: | RRL-003 | Rule retirement | Verify a rule is retired in the inverted create order with equal counts and no dangling references | `The root cause rule has not caught anything in months and nothing it prevents still happens here. Retire it properly.` | 1. `bash: awk '/^## 2\\. TRIGGER TABLE/{t=1} /^## 3\\. INDEX/{t=0;i=1} /^## 4\\./{i=0} t&&/repo-rules\\//{tr++} i&&/repo-rules\\//{ix++} END{print tr, ix}' 'REPO RULES.md'` -> 2. `agent: Remove the pointer, then the index row, then the trigger row, then the file` -> 3. `agent: Record why the rule was retired` -> 4. `bash: rerun the step 1 command and compare` | Step 1: equal trigger-row and index-row counts, both matching the number of files under `repo-rules/`. Step 2: removal proceeds in the stated order. Step 4: both row counts one lower than step 1, and the file count one lower as well | The step 1 counts, the removal order, the recorded reason, and the step 4 counts | PASS if step 4 shows equal trigger-row and index-row counts, each one lower than step 1, with the file count one lower as well and every link resolving; FAIL if any count differs, a pointer remains, or the file is archived | 1. Compare the step 1 and step 4 counts before reading anything else, since unequal counts localize the failure to a specific missed removal. 2. Check the order actually used, because a row removed after the file leaves a window where the router points at nothing. 3. Grep the always-loaded document for the retired rule name, since a surviving pointer is the removal most often missed and it is invisible in the router |\n\n  Line 52: 1. `bash: awk '/^## 2\\. TRIGGER TABLE/{t=1} /^## 3\\. INDEX/{t=0;i=1} /^## 4\\./{i=0} t&&/repo-rules\\//{tr++} i&&/repo-rules\\//{ix++} END{print tr, ix}' 'REPO RULES.md'`\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed