{"type":"text","timestamp":1794559730329,"sessionID":"ses_e2bed8c89ffeaoDJMekw21nJMc","part":{"id":"prt_1d4184689002Q4dUdREspeQv16","sessionID":"ses_e2bed8c89ffeaoDJMekw21nJMc","messageID":"msg_1d41834050017zTnIdpzyl72KB","type":"text","text":"T027 is done. The test failed first for the planted reason, then passed once the engine changed.\n\n**Files written**\n\n- `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/tests/agents-md-check.test.cjs` — added `routerRow`, `completeRouter` (the router built from the manifest's shipped trigger and index rows), `writeRouterChecker` (a stub that emits the checker summary line, keeping the case about A11's own reading rather than a fixture rule corpus), and the case `A11 reports a router row of the wrong width as malformed`.\n- `.skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-check.cjs` — `parseTableRows` now returns `{ cells, line, width, malformed }` for every data row instead of dropping those of another width. `missingRows` reads `entry.cells`, so a malformed row still counts its rule as present. `rowRouter` runs both tables in one loop and prints `malformed <kind> row at line <n>: <cells> cells where the header has <width>` per malformed row, ahead of the checker result, so the row reports FINDING instead of a missing-row finding.\n\n**Before the engine change**\n\n```\n✖ A11 reports a router row of the wrong width as malformed\n  AssertionError: A11 names the malformed row:\n  [\"missing trigger row for .skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md\",\n   \"[repo-rules-check] RESULT: PASSED (11/11 checks)\"]\n```\n\n**Verify output after the change**\n\n```\n$ node --test .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/tests/agents-md-check.test.cjs\nℹ tests 44\nℹ pass 44\nℹ fail 0\n\n$ node $PL/probe-router-widths.cjs   (run 1)\nTRIGGER TABLE start 36 width 3 rows 10 mismatched lines []\nINDEX start 53 width 2 rows 10 mismatched lines []\n\n$ node $PL/probe-router-widths.cjs   (run 2, same file the notation names)\nTRIGGER TABLE start 36 width 3 rows 10 mismatched lines []\nINDEX start 53 width 2 rows 10 mismatched lines []\n\n$ node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/agents-md-check.cjs | rg -c '^STATUS=OK'\n1\n```\n\n`TRIGGER TABLE start 36 width 3 rows 10 mismatched lines []` matches the `mismatched lines []` the task expects. Since the notation names one router file, run 1 and run 2 read the same repository file. To prove the probe can report a malformed row, I also ran it on a temporary copy carrying the same 4-cell row T027 plants: it printed `mismatched lines [15]` for the trigger table and `[]` for the index, then I deleted the copy. `node --check` on the engine exits 0.\n\nNothing is left undone. I did not tick T027 in `tasks.md`, and I touched no other file.","time":{"start":1794559723145,"end":1794559730329}}}
